//
// A generic whose parameter or return is a keyed collection over its type variable
// (`hash<T[key]>`, `sorted<T[key]>`) is refused at its declaration, saying why
// (`interfaces.md (G-Gen)`, loft#1538).
//
// A key names a FIELD of the element, and a type variable has no fields until it is
// instantiated, so no monomorph can resolve one.  The declaration used to compile and then drop
// out of every call's candidates: each call reported `Unknown function put_all` and nothing
// pointed at the declaration.  The refusal names the declaration and the cure, and a call to
// the refused function adds no second message.  C110 keeps keyed collections record sets, so
// this stays a refusal rather than a feature.
//
// @falsified-at: b12e15474 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/2 -> 0/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/2 -> 0/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1538-a-keyed-collection-over-a-type-variable-is-refused-at-its-declaration.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/2 -> 0/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/2 -> 0/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
//   CHANNEL: expectations, both backends.
//   ARMED: nothing.
//   WITNESS: on the control neither refusal is emitted (FAIL/2).  Measured as a probe on the
//     same build, a call to `put_all` reports `Unknown function put_all` and nothing points at
//     the declaration.
//   HOLDS: the program is refused on both trees; the message is what moves.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: parameter `h` is a keyed collection over the type variable T, and its key names a field that T does not have until it is instantiated — declare the collection over a concrete record type (`hash<Entry[key]>`)
// @EXPECT_ERROR: the return type is a keyed collection over the type variable T, and its key names a field that T does not have until it is instantiated — declare the collection over a concrete record type (`hash<Entry[key]>`)

struct Entry { key: text, val: integer }

fn put_all<T>(h: hash<T[key]>, e: T) -> integer { h += [e]; len(h) }

fn one<T>(e: T) -> sorted<T[key]> { s: sorted<T[key]> = [e]; s }

fn main() {
  h: hash<Entry[key]> = [];
  println("{put_all(h, Entry { key: "x", val: 1 })}");
}
